    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: c9701f6c47f4cf2469237abe2c58ae75, type: 3}
    titleName: Creating ISO Image File
    description: "Introduction\r\n \r\nAn ISO file is essentially a digital replica
      of an entire optical disc (CD, DVD, Blu-ray) compressed into a single file.
      This \"ISO image\" serves as a smaller, more manageable copy of the original
      disc's contents.\r\n \r\nHow ISO Files Work:\r\nTo utilize the data within
      an ISO file, it needs to be \"mounted\", meaning it's virtually opened and
      accessed as if it were the original physical disc.\r\n \r\nUses of ISO Files:\r\n\xB7        
      Data Backup: ISO files are commonly used for backing up optical discs, ensuring
      data preservation in a compact and readily accessible format.\r\n\xB7        
      Data Storage: They offer a convenient and efficient way to store large datasets,
      like game files or operating systems, in a single, portable file.\r\n\xB7        
      Disc Replication: ISO files are the primary tool for cloning optical discs,
      allowing users to create identical copies of their content. This is useful
      for replacing damaged discs or transferring data from old discs to newer ones.\r\n\xB7        
      Operating System Distribution: ISO files play a vital role in the distribution
      of operating systems (OS). They provide a single, comprehensive file containing
      all necessary OS components for download, installation, or burning onto a physical
      disc.\r\n \r\n \r\nHow to Create ISO image File\r\n \r\nYou can easily create
      ISO images of a physical disk using the pre-installed tools that come with
      operating systems like MacOS and Linux.\r\nHowever, that is not the case with
      the Windows operating system, so you will need to use third-party apps. These
      apps can be found all over the internet so you can pick and choose the one
      that works best for you. (e.g. PowerISO, ImgBurn, Rufus)\r\nFor this lesson,
      we will utilize the Rufus Software to create an ISO Image of Windows 10 from
      a disk.\r\n \r\n \r\nMaterials, tools and Equipment to prepare:\r\n\xB7        
      A USB flash drive with good storage capacity; preferably 8GB or more.\r\n\xB7        
      Access to a computer running Windows 8 or Windows 10.\r\n\xB7         A CD
      or DVD containing .iso image of an operating system.\r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n
      \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n1. Download Rufus from this Website and install it.
      (https://rufus.ie/)\r\n2.  Run Rufus.\r\n3. Click SELECT dropdown and another
      window will pop upto  where you can select the the drive that holds the disc
      of your operating system. It will now appear on your rufus application.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: b2398cb2d61547a449d434fc401d085c, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: '4. Under the Partitioning Scheme select MBR and Under Target System
      select BIOS

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
