    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: b376110d03b266a46beaf6d96ad70c76, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: '5. Click Start to begin copying file from your disc to your thumb
      drive (or USB drive)

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 7ab4136c54a71d0418657d439de948ff, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: '6. After making iso file completes successfully, you should receive
      a message, "Creating image file completed successfully". You can open the created
      iso file with Rufus and browse files in the ISO file.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'File System Types

'
    description: "File systems are categorized based on their functionalities and
      underlying technologies. Here are some common types:\r\n\xB7         File Allocation
      Table (FAT)\r\nFAT is an old, simple, and reliable file system that was used
      widely in early Windows computers. It started with floppy disks and later worked
      with hard disks, evolving from an 8-bit system to FAT32. While it\u2019s still
      compatible with many systems, it doesn\u2019t perform as well as newer file
      systems.\r\n \r\n\xB7         Extended File Allocation Table (exFAT)\r\nexFAT
      is the improved version of FAT32, supporting larger storage and files. It\u2019s
      still simple and easy to use but is designed for bigger capacity and modern
      applications.\r\n \r\n\xB7         New Technology File System (NTFS)\r\nNTFS
      has been the default file system for Windows since the early 90s. It offers
      better performance, supports large files, and includes additional features
      for security and data integrity. It\u2019s compatible with Linux and Mac in
      read-only mode.\r\n \r\n\xB7         Resilient File System (ReFS)\r\nReFS is
      a newer file system from Microsoft, designed for better scalability and performance
      than NTFS. However, it doesn\u2019t support some NTFS features and requires
      more system resources.\r\n \r\n\xB7         Universal Disk Format (UDF)\r\nUDF
      is a universal file system for optical media like DVDs and Blu-ray discs, replacing
      the older ISO 9660 system. It\u2019s widely used for storing video and audio
      content on discs.\r\n \r\n \r\nCreating Bootable Device\r\nWHY IS BOOTING REQUIRED?\r\n\xB7        
      Hardware doesn\u2019t know where the operating system resides and how to load
      it.\r\n\xB7         Need a special program to do this job \u2013 Bootstrap
      loader.\r\n(e.g. BIOS \u2013 Boot Input Output System)\r\n\xB7         Bootstrap
      loader locates the kernel, loads it into main memory and starts its execution.\r\n\xB7        
      In some systems, a simple bootstrap loader fetches a more complex boot program
      from disk, which in turn loads the kernel.\r\n \r\nBootable Devices\r\n1.   
      Booting is a startup sequence that launches the operating system of a computer
      when it is turned on.\r\n2.    A boot sequence is the initial set of operations
      that the computer performs when it is switched on. Every computer has a boot
      sequence.\r\n3.    A boot device is any device or drive that has the required
      boot files that allows the computer to start.\r\n \r\nIn this case, if we are
      building a PC from scratch, usually there is still no operating system. With
      this, an operating system can be booted or installed from a boot device.\r\n
      \r\nA boot drive commonly used is the hard drive. Usually, an operating system
      like Microsoft Windows is installed in a hard drive to load Windows.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
